    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Yes. A true HEPA filter removes airborne mold spores very effectively, because most spores are roughly 1 to 30 microns across or larger and HEPA is rated to catch at least 99.97 percent of particles at 0.3 microns, the hardest size to trap. What it cannot do is reach mold that is still growing on a wall, under a carpet or inside a cavity, kill the colonies producing those spores, or remove the musty smell, which is a gas and needs activated carbon. A HEPA purifier lowers the spore count in the air you breathe while you find and fix the moisture problem. It is a supporting tool, never the fix.
        </p>

        <div id="spore-size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Big Are Mold Spores Compared With What HEPA Catches?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mold spores are large by filtration standards. Most of the common indoor and outdoor molds release spores somewhere between about 2 and 20 microns, and some, like Alternaria, produce spores that are considerably longer than that. For comparison, a human hair is roughly 50 to 70 microns wide. A filter that can stop a 0.3 micron particle has very little trouble with something ten or fifty times that size.
          </p>

          <p>
            The 0.3 micron figure in the HEPA definition is not the smallest particle a HEPA filter can catch. It is close to the size that slips through fibrous filters most easily, sometimes called the most penetrating particle size. Larger particles are caught by impaction and interception, smaller ones by diffusion, and the dip in efficiency sits in between. So a filter that meets the US Department of Energy HEPA standard of 99.97 percent at 0.3 microns does better than that on whole mold spores.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mold does not only shed whole spores. Growing colonies also release fragments of spores and hyphae, and some of those fragments are well under a micron. They still fall inside the range a HEPA filter handles well, which is one reason HEPA is the filtration standard used in professional mold remediation equipment such as air scrubbers and HEPA vacuums.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Particle</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Rough Size</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">HEPA Capture</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Small spores (Aspergillus, Penicillium)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 2 to 5 microns</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Very high</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Mid-size spores (Cladosporium)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 3 to 15 microns</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Very high</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Large spores (Alternaria)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 20 microns and longer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Very high</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Spore and hyphal fragments</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Under 1 micron to a few microns</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Musty odor (MVOCs)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Gas molecules</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None; needs carbon</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The practical takeaway is that filter efficiency is not the weak link. A true HEPA filter will catch nearly every spore that passes through it. The questions that decide whether you notice a difference are how much of the room's air actually passes through the filter each hour, whether the spores are in the air at all, and whether the source is still producing more.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="airborne-only" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. If HEPA Catches Spores, Why Doesn't It Solve a Mold Problem?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Because an air purifier only cleans air that reaches it. Mold lives on surfaces: drywall, wood framing, carpet backing, ceiling tiles, the back of a dresser against a cold wall. The colony itself never leaves that surface, and most of the spores it produces settle nearby rather than drifting across the room into the purifier's intake. A HEPA filter in the middle of the bedroom does nothing for a patch of growth behind the headboard.
          </p>

          <p>
            Spore release is also uneven. Colonies tend to release spores in bursts when they are disturbed, when air moves across them, or when conditions change. Between those bursts the airborne count can be close to normal outdoor levels even in a house with a real problem. That is why a purifier can show clean air on its particle sensor while the closet still smells like a basement.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then there is the source. A leaking pipe, a damp crawl space or a bathroom that never dries out will keep feeding the growth no matter how many spores you filter. The purifier ends up working against a supply that never stops. The EPA's core guidance on mold is blunt about this: the key to mold control is moisture control, and the mold must be cleaned up and the water problem fixed or it will come back.
          </p>

          <p>
            So treat the HEPA filter as damage limitation. It reduces what you breathe in the room where it runs. It does not shrink the colony, dry the wall or tell you where the mold is. If you are buying one because you suspect mold, the purchase that matters more is often a moisture meter, a dehumidifier or a plumber.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="kill" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Does a HEPA Filter Kill the Mold Spores It Traps?
          </h2>

          <p>
            No. A HEPA filter is a mechanical trap. Spores caught in the fiber mat are held there, but they are not killed. Many mold spores are built to survive dry, unfavorable conditions for long periods and will stay viable in the filter until it is thrown away. In a reasonably dry room that is not a problem, because dormant spores in a filter do not hurt anyone.
          </p>

          <p>
            It becomes a problem in two situations. The first is high humidity. A filter loaded with dust and organic debris in a room that stays damp for weeks can give trapped spores enough moisture to grow, which is covered in detail in our guide to <a href="/can-mold-grow-in-an-air-purifier/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">whether mold can grow in an air purifier</a>. The second is filter changes. Pulling a loaded filter out and tapping it on the bin releases a cloud of exactly what you spent months collecting.
          </p>

          <p>
            Manufacturers sometimes add features that claim to kill what the filter catches, such as small UV-C lamps or antimicrobial coatings on the filter media. Treat those as minor extras. A UV lamp inside a purifier sees each particle for a fraction of a second, and an antimicrobial coating is mostly there to keep the filter itself from growing things. Neither turns the purifier into a mold treatment, and neither is a reason to pay much more.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Bag the old filter before it leaves the room.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">When you replace a filter from a purifier that has been running against mold, slide it straight into a plastic bag, seal it, and take it outside. Wear a mask that fits well if you are sensitive. Never vacuum or shake out a HEPA filter to reuse it.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            The upshot is that HEPA removes spores from circulation, not from existence. That is still useful. Fewer spores in the air means less exposure for anyone sensitive to them, and fewer spores landing on new damp surfaces to start new growth.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="smell" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Why Does the Musty Smell Stay After the Filter Is Running?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Because the smell is not spores. The musty, earthy odor of mold comes from microbial volatile organic compounds, usually shortened to MVOCs, which growing mold gives off as it digests whatever it is living on. They are gas molecules, far smaller than any particle, and they pass straight through a HEPA filter no matter how good it is.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gases are handled by adsorption, which in home purifiers means activated carbon. Carbon has an enormous internal surface area that holds gas molecules as air passes through it. The amount of carbon matters a great deal. A thin carbon-dusted mesh sheet wrapped around a HEPA cartridge does little for a persistent odor. A purifier for a musty room should carry a substantial bed of granular carbon, often measured in pounds rather than grams.
          </p>

          <p>
            Carbon also fills up. Once its surface is saturated it stops adsorbing, and the smell returns even though the filter looks clean. In a home with a steady odor source that can happen within a few months, which is why carbon stages usually need replacing more often than the HEPA layer. Our guide to <a href="/how-often-to-change-air-purifier-filter/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how often to change an air purifier filter</a> covers the schedules and the signs.
          </p>

          <p>
            More importantly, the smell is information. A persistent musty odor means mold is actively growing somewhere, even if you cannot see it. Masking it with a carbon filter, or with fragrance, removes the most useful clue you have for finding the source. Use the smell to track the problem down first, then use carbon to clear what lingers after the cleanup.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="true-hepa" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Is Every HEPA Filter Good Enough for Mold Spores?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Only filters that meet the actual standard. In the US, true HEPA means at least 99.97 percent efficiency at 0.3 microns, the Department of Energy definition. Product listings also use terms like HEPA-type, HEPA-style, 99 percent HEPA or HEPA-like, which have no fixed meaning. Some of those filters are decent; some are little better than a furnace filter. If a listing does not say true HEPA or quote the 99.97 percent at 0.3 microns figure, assume it does not meet it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Even a genuine HEPA filter only works if air goes through it rather than around it. Cheap housings with loose gaskets let air leak past the filter edge, and that bypass can matter more than the media rating. This is hard to see on a spec sheet, which is one reason the independently tested clean air delivery rate, or CADR, is a better guide than the filter label alone. CADR measures the clean air actually coming out of the machine.
          </p>

          <p>
            For mold, the CADR also tells you whether the unit is big enough. A small unit with an excellent filter that only moves 60 cubic feet per minute cannot keep up with a 300 square foot basement room. Our explainer on <a href="/what-size-air-purifier-do-i-need/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">what size air purifier you need</a> walks through the math, and the short version is to aim for a smoke CADR of at least two-thirds of the room's floor area in square feet, more for a problem space.
          </p>

          <p>
            Washable permanent HEPA filters deserve caution in a mold setting. Washing a fibrous HEPA mat can damage it, and a filter that goes back into the machine before it is completely dry is an invitation for growth. For a purifier running against mold, replaceable sealed filters are the safer choice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="use-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Should You Use a HEPA Purifier Against Mold?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Size it to the room and run it continuously. Spores are released intermittently, so a purifier that runs an hour a day misses most of them. Auto mode or a low to medium setting around the clock does more than short blasts on high. Close the door to the room if you can, so the unit is cleaning a defined volume rather than the whole floor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Put it where the air is worst, but not against the mold. In a basement, place it out in the open in the main living area, with a foot or two of clearance around the intake and outlet. Do not aim it at a moldy wall, which can blow spores off the surface, and do not use it as an excuse to leave visible growth in place.
          </p>

          <p>
            Turn it up during cleanup. Scrubbing or removing moldy material releases far more spores than the colony does on its own. Running a HEPA purifier on high in the work area during and after the job, with the room closed off, catches part of that release. Professional crews use larger HEPA air scrubbers for the same reason. Our <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-mold/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best air purifiers for mold</a> are chosen for real HEPA filtration, honest CADR and a meaningful carbon stage.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pair it with humidity control. The EPA recommends keeping indoor relative humidity below 60 percent, ideally between 30 and 50 percent. A purifier does nothing to humidity, which is why many damp basements need a dehumidifier first and a purifier second. Our comparison of an <a href="/air-purifier-vs-dehumidifier/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">air purifier vs a dehumidifier</a> explains which one solves which part of the problem.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="source" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. When Is a HEPA Purifier Not the Answer?
          </h2>

          <p>
            When there is visible growth larger than a small patch. The EPA says a homeowner can usually handle mold covering less than about 10 square feet, roughly a 3 by 3 foot area, as long as the moisture problem is fixed. Beyond that, or where the growth is in an HVAC system or came from sewage or contaminated water, the agency recommends following its remediation guidance and considering a professional. Our guide to <a href="/mold-remediation-cost/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">mold remediation cost</a> shows what that typically involves and costs.
          </p>

          <p>
            When the smell is strong but the mold is hidden. Musty odor with no visible source often means growth inside a wall, under flooring or in a crawl space. A purifier will not find it. Look for the moisture first: plumbing leaks, condensation on cold exterior walls, a wet crawl space, poor bathroom ventilation or water coming through a basement wall. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-get-rid-of-mold-in-basement/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how to get rid of mold in a basement</a> covers the most common source.
          </p>

          <p>
            When someone's health is involved. Mold can trigger allergy and asthma symptoms, and people with weakened immune systems or chronic lung disease can be more vulnerable. A HEPA purifier is a reasonable step to reduce exposure, but it is not a treatment. If anyone in the house has symptoms you think are linked to mold, talk to your doctor, and fix the source rather than relying on filtration.
          </p>

          <p>
            And when someone is selling an ozone generator as a mold solution. Ozone at levels safe to breathe does little to mold, and at levels that affect mold it is harmful to people. HEPA filtration plus moisture control plus proper cleanup is the combination the EPA and remediation industry actually use.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running it instead of finding the leak</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A HEPA purifier can run for a year in a room with a leaking pipe behind the wall and the mold will keep growing. Spend the first effort on finding moisture with a meter, your nose and a flashlight. Filter the air while you fix it, not as a substitute for fixing it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a HEPA-type filter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Filters labeled HEPA-type or HEPA-style have no required performance and some catch far fewer fine particles. For mold, insist on true HEPA, meaning at least 99.97 percent at 0.3 microns, and check the independently tested CADR so you know the whole machine, not just the filter, performs.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting it to remove the smell</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The musty odor is made of gases, not spores, and passes straight through HEPA. Without a substantial carbon stage the smell will stay. Even with carbon, a persistent odor means active growth, so use the smell to locate the source before you treat it as a filtering problem.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pointing it at the moldy wall</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Blowing air across a mold colony can knock spores loose and spread them around the room. Place the purifier in the open with clear space around it, keep the outlet aimed away from any growth, and close off the area while the visible mold is cleaned or removed.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Shaking out the old filter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A filter that has been catching mold spores for months is full of them. Tapping it out, vacuuming it or rinsing it puts them right back into the air. Bag it in the room, seal the bag, take it outside and replace it with a new sealed filter.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a HEPA filter catch black mold spores?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Stachybotrys chartarum, the mold often called black mold, produces spores in the same general size range as other molds, several microns across, which a true HEPA filter traps very efficiently. Its spores are sticky and not easily airborne unless the growth is disturbed. The real risk is the damp material it grows on, which needs to be removed, not filtered.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a HEPA purifier stop mold from spreading?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It reduces the number of spores in the air that could land on other damp surfaces, which helps a little. Mold spreads mainly where moisture allows it, though. If the humidity is high or a surface stays wet, spores already present will grow. Keeping humidity in the 30 to 50 percent range does more to stop spread than filtration alone.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a HEPA filter better than a UV light for mold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For airborne spores, yes. HEPA physically removes spores from the air every time air passes through. A UV lamp inside a purifier gives each spore a very brief exposure, and mold spores are relatively resistant to UV. For surface mold, neither is the answer; cleaning and moisture control are. UV can help keep coils and drip pans clean in HVAC systems.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does it take a HEPA purifier to clear mold spores from a room?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A correctly sized purifier gives about four to five air changes an hour, which removes most airborne particles within 30 to 60 minutes with the door closed, as long as nothing is releasing more. With an active colony nearby, the count drops but never reaches zero, because spores keep coming. That is why fixing the source matters.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a HEPA furnace filter remove mold spores too?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most homes cannot use a true HEPA filter in the furnace because it restricts airflow too much for a standard blower. A MERV 11 to 13 filter, if your system can handle it, still catches a large share of mold spores from the air that passes through the ducts. A portable HEPA purifier in the affected room adds a higher standard of filtration where it counts.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can mold spores pass through a HEPA filter at all?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A small fraction can. True HEPA is rated at 99.97 percent at 0.3 microns, and capture is higher for larger particles, but leaks around the filter edge, damaged media or a poorly sealed housing let some air bypass the filter entirely. That bypass, not the filter media, is usually the main reason a purifier underperforms against spores.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I run a HEPA purifier during mold cleanup?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Disturbing moldy material releases a large burst of spores, and a HEPA purifier on high in the closed-off work area catches part of it. Professionals use bigger HEPA air scrubbers, often under negative pressure, for the same reason. Wear protection such as an N95 respirator, gloves and goggles, and follow EPA cleanup guidance for the size of the job.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

