        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A true HEPA filter is excellent at what it does. Mold spores are large, HEPA catches them almost completely, and a correctly sized purifier running continuously will cut the number floating in a room. That is a real benefit for anyone sensitive to mold, and it is why HEPA filtration is part of every professional remediation job.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            But spores in the air are the symptom. The mold is on a surface somewhere, fed by moisture you have not fixed yet. Find the water, clean or remove the growth, bring humidity down to the 30 to 50 percent range, and use a HEPA purifier with a real carbon stage to clean up what is left in the air. In that order, it works.
          </p>
        </div>

